---
description: Treat context as expensive. Delegate read-heavy work, pipe output, keep responses concise.
alwaysApply: true
---

## Context Cost Awareness

- **You are an autoregressive model. Every turn re-sends the full conversation as input.** Large tool outputs (file reads, command output, logs) land in context and inflate the cost of every subsequent turn permanently until compaction. This compounds fast on expensive models.
- **Delegate read-heavy and output-heavy work to subagents.** When a task involves reading large files, parsing logs, running commands that produce significant output, or any grunt work a cheaper model handles equally well: spawn a subagent (Claude Code: pass `model: "sonnet"` for the cheaper model; Cursor: use a subagent worktree). The subagent gets a fresh, tiny context, does the work, and returns only what matters.
- **Prompt caches expire, and an expired cache re-bills the whole context.** In Claude Code a subagent's cache lasts 5 minutes idle and the main session's lasts 1 hour. The first turn after a longer gap re-sends the entire context at the cache-write price, so cost grows with how long agents live, how big their context gets, and how often they sit waiting.
  - Subagents get short, bounded briefs measured in minutes, not hours: edit, run only the tests they touch, report. They must never wait on builds, full suites, locks, deploys or live runs; the main session runs those as background shells.
  - Never resume a large-context agent after a gap. Do the fix on the main thread or brief a fresh agent tightly.
  - The main session must not park behind multi-hour agents either, because every wake-up after an hour idle re-reads its full context uncached. Split long work into short rounds that report back within the hour, and never launch an agent expected to run for many hours.
- **Never read large log files, transcripts, or debug output directly on the main thread.** A `cost-guard` hook enforces this. If it blocks you, delegate to a subagent with the cheaper model.
- **Pipe commands to limit output.** When running commands directly, always pipe through `head`, `tail`, `grep`, or `wc` to cap what enters context. Never let unbounded output flow into the main thread.
- **Keep responses concise.** Your response becomes part of the context that gets re-sent on every future turn. Verbosity today costs money on every turn tomorrow.
